  "query_preview": "..Satellite imagery this morning showed leading edge\nof high level clouds associated with post-tropical cyclone\nDouglas which was located about 1200 miles west of the southern\ntip of Baja CA. These high level clouds will overspread the area\ntoday with additional mid-level clouds increasing later this \nafternoon into this evening as PWAT values increase to 0.50\" \neast to almost 1\" western areas. Highs today will be at or a few\ndegrees warmer than yesterday. \n\nTonight: Weak upper level trof from remnant Douglas will lift\nacross the western half of the state, likely developing virga\nwest of Tucson. Not expecting any measurable rain with the low-\nlevels rather dry. \n\nSaturday: As the country celebrates its 250th birthday, there\nwill be plenty of mainly mid to high level clouds over the \narea. PWATs will continue to slowly increase. Highs at or a few \ndegrees cooler than today.\n\nSunday and Monday: Warmer as the upper high over New Mexico\nSunday drifts west across eastern AZ on Monday. Chance of storms\nnear the AZ/NM border on Monday.\n\nTuesday through Thursday: Shower and thunderstorm chances will\ngradually daily spread west during this period but mainly be \nfrom Tucson east and south. At this time, highs are forecast to \nbe 2 to 6 degrees above normal.",
